{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>For you were strangers in the land of Egypt</b>. It is not the case that all strangers are rendered fit subjects of kindness merely because we were once strangers in a land for a time, nor is there any reason for them to be assured forever on account of that. Rashi explained that it is the reason for לֹא תוֹנוּ אֹתוֹ (\"you shall not wrong him\"): he warns that you must not wrong him with הוֹנָאַת דְּבָרִים (verbal oppression), for if you oppressed him, he too could oppress you and say to you, \"You too came as a stranger\" — a blemish that is in you, do not declare about your fellow. And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said: \"Remember that you were strangers, just like him.\" But none of this constitutes the essential reason. The correct interpretation in my eyes is as follows: it says, you shall not wrong a stranger and you shall not oppress him, and you might suppose that he has no one to deliver him from your hand, but you know that you were strangers in the land of Egypt, and I saw the oppression with which the Egyptians oppressed you, and I took vengeance upon them, for I see the tears of the oppressed who have no comforter, and from the hand of their oppressors they have no strength, and I deliver every person from the hand of one stronger than he — and likewise, the widow and the orphan you shall not afflict, for I will hear their cry, since all these do not trust in themselves but place their trust in Me. And in the other verse He adds a further reason: \"And you know the soul of the stranger, for you were strangers in the land of Egypt\" (Exodus 23:9). That is to say, you know that the soul of every stranger is cast down within him, and he sighs and cries out, and his eyes are always toward HaShem, and He will have compassion upon him just as He had compassion upon you, as it is written: \"And the children of Israel sighed from the servitude, and they cried out, and their cry ascended to the Elohim from the servitude\" (Exodus 2:23) — that is to say, not by their merit, but rather He had compassion upon them because of the servitude."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:15.410314873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}